# Look
option.LOOK_PRESET=Look
option.LOOK_PRESET.comment=Picks how light and color are rendered. Natural: a plain Minecraft-like look that is easy on the eyes. Vivid: like SEUS Renewed / PTGI, with contrast between sun and shade, warm sunlight against blue-green shade, green bounce light from the grass and highlights that reach near white (saturation and bloom stay modest). Daylight: Vivid lighting plus a deeper blue sky, greener grass and more sunlit relief, for punchy clear days. Summer Sky: bright, airy midsummer days with deep blue down to the horizon, lush greens and warm sun. Golden Hour: warm, low-sun light all day with soft bloom. Teal & Orange: the classic film look, shade and grass toward teal, highlights, wood and soil toward orange. Bleach Bypass: muted colors and hard contrast (strong contrast, tiring over long sessions). Cross Process: yellow-green highlights and blue shadows (strong color and contrast). Game: clear, saturated colors with lifted dark areas for easy play. Saturation, Contrast, Exposure Compensation, Bloom, Bloomy Fog, Sky Light Occlusion, Sky Ambient Saturation and Fog Density multiply the preset values (exposure is added). Color Grading settings also stack on top of the preset. Custom: set the preset-only values under Post Processing > Custom Look
value.LOOK_PRESET.0=Custom
value.LOOK_PRESET.1=Natural
value.LOOK_PRESET.2=Vivid
value.LOOK_PRESET.3=Daylight
value.LOOK_PRESET.4=Summer Sky
value.LOOK_PRESET.5=Golden Hour
value.LOOK_PRESET.6=Teal & Orange
value.LOOK_PRESET.7=Bleach Bypass
value.LOOK_PRESET.8=Cross Process
value.LOOK_PRESET.9=Game (Vivid)
option.LOOK_LUT=LUT
option.LOOK_LUT.comment=A color LUT applied last (3D LUT on the display values after the tone map). Stacks after the look preset and Color Grading. User LUT: replace shaders/textures/look/user_lut.png with your own (the identity image graded in an image editor, or a .cube converted with tools/gen_look_lut.py; see Look in the README). Sun and Shade Split: warmer sun, bluer-green shade, bright greens toward yellow-green. Film: slightly lower contrast, warm and less saturated highlights. The film-like LUTs approximate the characteristics described in the makers' public data sheets; no manufacturer data is used. Portra-like: lower contrast, lifted blacks, soft warm color. Ektar-like: vivid reds and blues, firm contrast. Vision3 500T-like: the blue cast of tungsten cinema film used in daylight, soft gradation. Velvia-like: high saturation and contrast, deep blue skies and greens (strong color and contrast). Provia-like: faithful, slightly vivid. Eterna-like: calm low saturation and soft contrast
